{
  "id": "c15",
  "title": "Structs",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "c15-l1",
      "title": "Grouping Values",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "A struct groups related values into one new type: struct point { int x; int y; }; Every struct point now has an x and a y.",
        "Access the parts, called members, with a dot: p.x = 3;. A struct can be filled right away: struct point p = {3, 4};",
        "Unix itself is full of structs: every file has a struct stat with its size, owner and dates."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "struct point {",
          "    int x;",
          "    int y;",
          "};",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    struct point a = {3, 4};",
          "    struct point b;",
          "    b.x = a.x + 10;",
          "    b.y = a.y * 2;",
          "    printf(\"a = (%d, %d)\\n\", a.x, a.y);",
          "    printf(\"b = (%d, %d)\\n\", b.x, b.y);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "a = (3, 4)",
          "b = (13, 8)"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
