    {
      "id": "c15-l2",
      "title": "Arrays of Structs",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "An array of structs is like a table with named columns: struct student class[3]; and class[1].grade is the grade of the second student.",
        "Members can be arrays too: char name[20]; holds a name inside the struct.",
        "Loops over arrays of structs are how early databases worked: payrolls, phone books, inventories."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "struct student {",
          "    char name[20];",
          "    int grade;",
          "};",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    struct student c[3] = {{\"ada\", 92}, {\"ken\", 78}, {\"dmr\", 88}};",
          "    int i, best = 0;",
          "    for (i = 1; i < 3; i++)",
          "        if (c[i].grade > c[best].grade)",
          "            best = i;",
          "    printf(\"best: %s with %d\\n\", c[best].name, c[best].grade);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "best: ada with 92"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
