    {
      "id": "c15-l3",
      "title": "Pointers to Structs",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Big structs are usually passed to functions as pointers, so nothing has to be copied, and the function can change them.",
        "With a pointer p to a struct, (*p).x is the member x. Because that's so common, C has a shortcut: p->x.",
        "Pointers to structs are the building blocks of everything that comes next: linked lists, trees, and the data structures inside Unix."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "struct account {",
          "    char owner[20];",
          "    int balance;",
          "};",
          "",
          "void deposit(struct account *a, int amount) {",
          "    a->balance += amount;",
          "}",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    struct account acc = {\"ritchie\", 100};",
          "    deposit(&acc, 50);",
          "    printf(\"%s: %d\\n\", acc.owner, acc.balance);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "ritchie: 150"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c15-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does p->x mean?",
          "options": [
            "(*p).x: member x of the struct p points to",
            "p minus x",
            "A pointer to x",
            "p.x"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The arrow is shorthand for following the pointer and taking a member."
        },
        {
          "id": "c15-l3-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why pass a struct as a pointer?",
          "options": [
            "No copying, and the function can change it",
            "Pointers are required for structs",
            "To make it smaller",
            "It's slower otherwise… no reason"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Both reasons matter for big records."
        },
